course: dictionary
lesson: 5595
title: Lick: one word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Lick usually means to move your tongue over something. The words around it show you which meaning is right. «The dog licked my hand to say hello.» A dog has a tongue, and a friendly dog is saying hello, so lick is about the tongue.
explain: Lick can also mean to touch something lightly, and it has more than one meaning. «Flames licked at the stone walls of the old hall.» Flames have no tongues, so they must be touching the walls lightly, the way a tongue would. Test it: put your meaning into the sentence and check that it still makes sense.
explain: Lick can be a noun too. «He put a lick of paint on the fence.» Here a lick is a thin coat of paint, not a movement of the tongue. When a word has several meanings, ask which one fits the words around it.
fact: Lick (verb) means to move your tongue over something, to taste it, eat it or wet it.
fact: Flames lick something when they touch it lightly.
fact: A lick is one movement of the tongue, and a lick of paint is a thin coat of paint.
fact: Licking dirty fingers or sharing a licked spoon can spread germs, so wash your hands and use your own cutlery.
fact: Never lick batteries or metal poles in frost, because they are not food and can hurt you.
exercise: «Lick a lemon and it is sour.» What does lick mean in this sentence? | To squeeze hard until it is flat | *To touch and taste with your tongue | To blow on something to cool it down | To wash in a bowl of water
exercise: «Flames licked at the stone walls of the old hall.» What does licked suggest? | Pushed them over | Painted them a bright colour | *Touched them lightly, like a tongue | Crashed into them hard
exercise: «He put a lick of paint on the fence.» In this sentence, what is a lick? | A tool for cutting wood | A sticky sweet | A loud noise | *A thin coat of paint
test: Nadia says lick is only about dogs. Is she right? | Yes, only dogs lick anything | *No, people lick things too, and flames can lick walls | Yes, but only cats lick | No, lick means to shout
test: Alpine ibex climb the walls of dams to lick salt. What do they want from the wall? | To shelter from the rain | To drink the water | To find grass to eat | *Salt
exam: Why should you not lick batteries? | *They are not food and can harm you | Licking them makes your teeth grow | They taste sweet | They are too light to lick
